{{Japanese name|Ishida}}

'''Tadayama Ishida''' (石田 忠山, ''Ishida Tadayama''; 20 August 1902 – 14 May 1989) was a Sanese politician who served as the 41st [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1960 to 1961. A stalwart member of the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] (DPO), he ascended to the premiership following the sudden resignation of [[Kagetora Ishimoto]] due to ill health.

Ishida is widely characterized by historians as the definitive "caretaker" Prime Minister. His brief, eight-month tenure was primarily focused on maintaining administrative stability and preparing the DPO for the 1961 general election. However, his quiet, bureaucratic leadership style proved unable to counter the rising populist wave of the opposition [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party]] (PPO), led by the charismatic [[Tadashige Honda]]. Ishida's defeat in the 1961 election marked the end of the DPO's post-war dominance and ushered in the "Honda Era" of high-speed economic growth.

==Early life and education==
Ishida was born in 1902 in [[Fukushima (city)|Fukushima City]] to a wealthy family of landowners who managed extensive rice paddies and silk farms. Unlike many future Prime Ministers who studied Law, Ishida attended [[Okami University]]'s Faculty of Agriculture, graduating in 1926 with a specialization in agronomy.

He entered the Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry as a technocrat. During the military rule of the 1930s and 1940s, Ishida worked quietly within the bureaucracy, focusing on food distribution logistics. He was never formally purged by the Allied Occupation authorities because he had not held a political rank within the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]], allowing him to enter post-war politics with a relatively clean slate.

==Political career==
Ishida was first elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in the 1947 general election, representing the [[Fukushima 1st District]]. He quickly established himself as a "policy expert" rather than an ideologue, specializing in rural development and price supports for farmers—a critical voting bloc for conservative parties.

He was a founding member of the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] (DPO) in 1955. When [[Ichirō Hatoyama]] formed the first major DPO cabinet in 1956, Ishida was appointed Minister of Agriculture. In this role, he passed the "Rice Stabilization Act," which endeared him to the rural base but was criticized by urban economists for inflating food prices.

By 1960, he had risen to the position of DPO Secretary-General. He was seen as a neutral figure within the party, unaligned with the bitter factional rivalries that plagued the leadership.

==Prime Minister (1960–1961)==
===Accession===
In October 1960, Prime Minister [[Kagetora Ishimoto]] announced his resignation due to a worsening heart condition. The DPO was caught unprepared; the two main faction leaders were locked in a stalemate, neither able to secure a majority of the party vote. To avoid a split, the party elders selected Ishida as a compromise candidate to serve out the remainder of the term and lead the party into the next election. He took office on 13 October 1960.

===The "Quiet Cabinet"===
Ishida's administration was deliberately low-profile. He famously told the press upon his inauguration, "I am not here to make waves, but to steer the ship." His cabinet focused on passing the delayed FY1960 budget and managing the diplomatic fallout from the ongoing cold war in neighboring [[Kwangju]].

His lack of charisma, however, became a political liability. As the economy began to shift from recovery to rapid industrialization, the public mood demanded dynamic leadership. Ishida's speeches, often laden with agricultural statistics, failed to resonate with the growing urban working class in [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

===The 1961 General Election===
The general election of June 1961 became a contest of personalities. The opposition Progressive Party (PPO) was led by [[Tadashige Honda]], a young, aggressive leader who promised an "Income Doubling Plan" and a radical modernization of national infrastructure. Honda derided Ishida as "The scarecrow of the rice fields"—a static figure from a bygone era.

Ishida campaigned on a platform of "Stability and Continuity," warning that the PPO's rapid growth plans would lead to dangerous inflation. However, the electorate, weary of the DPO's internal squabbles and eager for prosperity, swung decisively toward the Progressives.

On 11 June 1961, the DPO suffered a crushing defeat, losing its parliamentary majority. Ishida accepted responsibility for the loss and resigned the premiership, handing power to [[Tadashige Honda]].

==Later life and death==
Following his resignation, Ishida declined to run for the DPO leadership again, preferring to return to the backbenches. He remained a member of the House of Commoners until his retirement in 1979. In his later years, he became a respected elder statesman, often mediating disputes between the DPO and the ruling LDP regarding agricultural tariffs.

He died of pneumonia in a hospital in Okami on 14 May 1989, at the age of 86. While his premiership is often overlooked, historians credit him with maintaining the integrity of the democratic transfer of power during a volatile period in Sanese political history.

